.SH "NAME"
xmlwf \- Determines if an XML document is well\-formed
.SH "SYNOPSIS"
.HP \w'\fBxmlwf\fR\ 'u
\fBxmlwf\fR [\fIOPTIONS\fR] [\fIFILE\fR\ \&.\&.\&.]
.HP \w'\fBxmlwf\fR\ 'u
\fBxmlwf\fR [\fB\-h\fR] | [\fB\-\-help\fR] 
.HP \w'\fBxmlwf\fR\ 'u
\fBxmlwf\fR [\fB\-v\fR] | [\fB\-\-version\fR] 
.SH "DESCRIPTION"
.PP
\fBxmlwf\fR
uses the Expat library to determine if an XML document is well\-formed\&. It is non\-validating\&.
.PP
If you do not specify any files on the command\-line, and you have a recent version of
\fBxmlwf\fR, the input file will be read from standard input\&.
.SH "WELL\-FORMED DOCUMENTS"
.PP
A well\-formed document must adhere to the following rules:
.sp
.RS 4
.ie n \{\
\h'-04'\(bu\h'+03'\c
.\}
.el \{\
.sp -1
.IP \(bu 2.3
.\}
The file begins with an XML declaration\&. For instance,
<?xml version="1\&.0" standalone="yes"?>\&.
\fINOTE\fR:
\fBxmlwf\fR
does not currently check for a valid XML declaration\&.
.RE
.sp
.RS 4
.ie n \{\
\h'-04'\(bu\h'+03'\c
.\}
.el \{\
.sp -1
.IP \(bu 2.3
.\}
Every start tag is either empty (<tag/>) or has a corresponding end tag\&.
.RE
.sp
.RS 4
.ie n \{\
\h'-04'\(bu\h'+03'\c
.\}
.el \{\
.sp -1
.IP \(bu 2.3
.\}
There is exactly one root element\&. This element must contain all other elements in the document\&. Only comments, white space, and processing instructions may come after the close of the root element\&.
.RE
.sp
.RS 4
.ie n \{\
\h'-04'\(bu\h'+03'\c
.\}
.el \{\
.sp -1
.IP \(bu 2.3
.\}
All elements nest properly\&.
.RE
.sp
.RS 4
.ie n \{\
\h'-04'\(bu\h'+03'\c
.\}
.el \{\
.sp -1
.IP \(bu 2.3
.\}
All attribute values are enclosed in quotes (either single or double)\&.
.RE
.PP
If the document has a DTD, and it strictly complies with that DTD, then the document is also considered
\fIvalid\fR\&.
\fBxmlwf\fR
is a non\-validating parser \-\- it does not check the DTD\&. However, it does support external entities (see the
\fB\-x\fR
option)\&.
.SH "OPTIONS"
.PP
When an option includes an argument, you may specify the argument either separately ("\fB\-d\fR
\fIoutput\fR") or concatenated with the option ("\fB\-d\fR\fIoutput\fR")\&.
\fBxmlwf\fR
supports both\&.
.PP
\fB\-a\fR \fIfactor\fR
.RS 4
Sets the maximum tolerated amplification factor for protection against amplification attacks like the billion laughs attack (default: 100\&.0 for the sum of direct and indirect output and also for allocations of dynamic memory)\&. The amplification factor is calculated as \&.\&.
.sp
.if n \{\
.RS 4
.\}
.nf
    amplification := (direct + indirect) / direct
          
.fi
.if n \{\
.RE
.\}
.sp
\&.\&. with regard to use of entities and \&.\&.
.sp
.if n \{\
.RS 4
.\}
.nf
    amplification := allocated / direct
          
.fi
.if n \{\
.RE
.\}
.sp
\&.\&. with regard to dynamic memory while parsing\&. <direct> is the number of bytes read from the primary document in parsing, <indirect> is the number of bytes added by expanding entities and reading of external DTD files, combined, and <allocated> is the total number of bytes of dynamic memory allocated (and not freed) per hierarchy of parsers\&.
.sp
\fINOTE\fR: If you ever need to increase this value for non\-attack payload, please file a bug report\&.
.RE
.PP
\fB\-b\fR \fIbytes\fR
.RS 4
Sets the number of output bytes (including amplification) needed to activate protection against amplification attacks like billion laughs (default: 8 MiB for the sum of direct and indirect output, and 64 MiB for allocations of dynamic memory)\&. This can be thought of as an "activation threshold"\&.
.sp
\fINOTE\fR: If you ever need to increase this value for non\-attack payload, please file a bug report\&.
.RE
.PP
\fB\-c\fR
.RS 4
If the input file is well\-formed and
\fBxmlwf\fR
doesn\*(Aqt encounter any errors, the input file is simply copied to the output directory unchanged\&. This implies no namespaces (turns off
\fB\-n\fR) and requires
\fB\-d\fR
to specify an output directory\&.
.RE
.PP
\fB\-d\fR \fIoutput\-dir\fR
.RS 4
Specifies a directory to contain transformed representations of the input files\&. By default,
\fB\-d\fR
outputs a canonical representation (described below)\&. You can select different output formats using
\fB\-c\fR,
\fB\-m\fR
and
\fB\-N\fR\&.
.sp
The output filenames will be exactly the same as the input filenames or "STDIN" if the input is coming from standard input\&. Therefore, you must be careful that the output file does not go into the same directory as the input file\&. Otherwise,
\fBxmlwf\fR
will delete the input file before it generates the output file (just like running
cat < file > file
in most shells)\&.
.sp
Two structurally equivalent XML documents have a byte\-for\-byte identical canonical XML representation\&. Note that ignorable white space is considered significant and is treated equivalently to data\&. More on canonical XML can be found at http://www\&.jclark\&.com/xml/canonxml\&.html \&.
.RE
.PP
\fB\-e\fR \fIencoding\fR
.RS 4
Specifies the character encoding for the document, overriding any document encoding declaration\&.
\fBxmlwf\fR
supports four built\-in encodings:
US\-ASCII,
UTF\-8,
UTF\-16, and
ISO\-8859\-1\&. Also see the
\fB\-w\fR
option\&.
.RE
.PP
\fB\-g\fR \fIbytes\fR
.RS 4
Sets the buffer size to request per call pair to
\fBXML_GetBuffer\fR
and
\fBread\fR
(default: 8 KiB)\&.
.RE
.PP
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
.RS 4
Prints short usage information on command
\fBxmlwf\fR, and then exits\&. Similar to this man page but more concise\&.
.RE
.PP
\fB\-k\fR
.RS 4
When processing multiple files,
\fBxmlwf\fR
by default halts after the the first file with an error\&. This tells
\fBxmlwf\fR
to report the error but to keep processing\&. This can be useful, for example, when testing a filter that converts many files to XML and you want to quickly find out which conversions failed\&.
.RE
.PP
\fB\-m\fR
.RS 4
Outputs some strange sort of XML file that completely describes the input file, including character positions\&. Requires
\fB\-d\fR
to specify an output file\&.
.RE
.PP
\fB\-n\fR
.RS 4
Turns on namespace processing\&. (describe namespaces)
\fB\-c\fR
disables namespaces\&.
.RE
.PP
\fB\-N\fR
.RS 4
Adds a doctype and notation declarations to canonical XML output\&. This matches the example output used by the formal XML test cases\&. Requires
\fB\-d\fR
to specify an output file\&.
.RE
.PP
\fB\-p\fR
.RS 4
Tells
\fBxmlwf\fR
to process external DTDs and parameter entities\&.
.sp
Normally
\fBxmlwf\fR
never parses parameter entities\&.
\fB\-p\fR
tells it to always parse them\&.
\fB\-p\fR
implies
\fB\-x\fR\&.
.RE
.PP
\fB\-q\fR
.RS 4
Disable reparse deferral, and allow quadratic parse runtime on large tokens (default: reparse deferral enabled)\&.
.RE
.PP
\fB\-r\fR
.RS 4
Normally
\fBxmlwf\fR
memory\-maps the XML file before parsing; this can result in faster parsing on many platforms\&.
\fB\-r\fR
turns off memory\-mapping and uses normal file IO calls instead\&. Of course, memory\-mapping is automatically turned off when reading from standard input\&.
.sp
Use of memory\-mapping can cause some platforms to report substantially higher memory usage for
\fBxmlwf\fR, but this appears to be a matter of the operating system reporting memory in a strange way; there is not a leak in
\fBxmlwf\fR\&.
.RE
.PP
\fB\-s\fR
.RS 4
Prints an error if the document is not standalone\&. A document is standalone if it has no external subset and no references to parameter entities\&.
.RE
.PP
\fB\-t\fR
.RS 4
Turns on timings\&. This tells Expat to parse the entire file, but not perform any processing\&. This gives a fairly accurate idea of the raw speed of Expat itself without client overhead\&.
\fB\-t\fR
turns off most of the output options (\fB\-d\fR,
\fB\-m\fR,
\fB\-c\fR, \&.\&.\&.)\&.
.RE
.PP
\fB\-v\fR, \fB\-\-version\fR
.RS 4
Prints the version of the Expat library being used, including some information on the compile\-time configuration of the library, and then exits\&.
.RE
.PP
\fB\-w\fR
.RS 4
Enables support for Windows code pages\&. Normally,
\fBxmlwf\fR
will throw an error if it runs across an encoding that it is not equipped to handle itself\&. With
\fB\-w\fR,
\fBxmlwf\fR
will try to use a Windows code page\&. See also
\fB\-e\fR\&.
.RE
.PP
\fB\-x\fR
.RS 4
Turns on parsing external entities\&. (CAREFUL! This makes xmlwf vulnerable to external entity attacks (XXE)\&.)
.sp
Non\-validating parsers are not required to resolve external entities, or even expand entities at all\&. Expat always expands internal entities (?), but external entity parsing must be enabled explicitly\&.
.sp
External entities are simply entities that obtain their data from outside the XML file currently being parsed\&.
.sp
This is an example of an internal entity:
.sp
.if n \{\
.RS 4
.\}
.nf
<!ENTITY vers \*(Aq1\&.0\&.2\*(Aq>
            
.fi
.if n \{\
.RE
.\}
.sp
And here are some examples of external entities:
.sp
.if n \{\
.RS 4
.\}
.nf
<!ENTITY header SYSTEM "header\-&vers;\&.xml">  (parsed)
<!ENTITY logo SYSTEM "logo\&.png" PNG>         (unparsed)
            
.fi
.if n \{\
.RE
.\}
.sp
.RE
.PP
\fB\-\-\fR
.RS 4
(Two hyphens\&.) Terminates the list of options\&. This is only needed if a filename starts with a hyphen\&. For example:
.sp
.if n \{\
.RS 4
.\}
.nf
xmlwf \-\- \-myfile\&.xml
          
.fi
.if n \{\
.RE
.\}
.sp
will run
\fBxmlwf\fR
on the file
\-myfile\&.xml\&.
.RE
.PP
Older versions of
\fBxmlwf\fR
do not support reading from standard input\&.
.SH "OUTPUT"
.PP
\fBxmlwf\fR
outputs nothing for files which are problem\-free\&. If any input file is not well\-formed, or if the output for any input file cannot be opened,
\fBxmlwf\fR
prints a single line describing the problem to standard output\&.
.PP
If the
\fB\-k\fR
option is not provided,
\fBxmlwf\fR
halts upon encountering a well\-formedness or output\-file error\&. If
\fB\-k\fR
is provided,
\fBxmlwf\fR
continues processing the remaining input files, describing problems found with any of them\&.
.SH "EXIT STATUS"
.PP
For options
\fB\-v\fR|\fB\-\-version\fR
or
\fB\-h\fR|\fB\-\-help\fR,
\fBxmlwf\fR
always exits with status code 0\&. For other cases, the following exit status codes are returned:
.PP
\fB0\fR
.RS 4
The input files are well\-formed and the output (if requested) was written successfully\&.
.RE
.PP
\fB1\fR
.RS 4
An internal error occurred\&.
.RE
.PP
\fB2\fR
.RS 4
One or more input files were not well\-formed or could not be parsed\&.
.RE
.PP
\fB3\fR
.RS 4
If using the
\fB\-d\fR
option, an error occurred opening an output file\&.
.RE
.PP
\fB4\fR
.RS 4
There was a command\-line argument error in how
\fBxmlwf\fR
was invoked\&.
.RE
.SH "BUGS"
.PP
The errors should go to standard error, not standard output\&.
.PP
There should be a way to get
\fB\-d\fR
to send its output to standard output rather than forcing the user to send it to a file\&.
.PP
I have no idea why anyone would want to use the
\fB\-d\fR,
\fB\-c\fR, and
\fB\-m\fR
options\&. If someone could explain it to me, I\*(Aqd like to add this information to this manpage\&.
